{
  "id": "imperativ",
  "name": "Imperatives",
  "level": "A1",
  "subtitle": "Use the base verb to tell someone what to do: Open the door. Add Don't to say what not to do: Don't run. Let's + verb makes a suggestion for everyone: Let's go.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "base verb",
    "Don't",
    "Let's"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "base verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "(please)",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "rest!"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Open</span> the door. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Don't</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">run</span>! · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Let's</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">go</span>!",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Use the <strong>base verb</strong>. There is no <em>you</em> and no <em>-s</em>: <em>Open the door. Sit down. Listen.</em> It is the same for one person or for many people.",
        "tag": "Base verb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Don't</strong> + base verb says what <em>not</em> to do: <em>Don't run. Don't touch it. Don't forget your keys.</em>",
        "tag": "Don't"
      },
      {
        "html": "With <strong>be</strong>, use <em>Be</em> + adjective: <em>Be quiet. Be careful.</em> The negative is <em>Don't be</em>: <em>Don't be late.</em>",
        "tag": "Be / Don't be"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Let's</strong> + base verb is a suggestion for you and me (<em>Let's</em> = <em>let us</em>): <em>Let's go. Let's have lunch.</em> The negative is <em>Let's not</em>: <em>Let's not wait.</em>",
        "tag": "Let's"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Please</strong> makes a command polite. Put it at the start or at the end (with a comma): <em>Please sit down. Sit down, please.</em> A name goes first or last, with a comma: <em>Amira, close the window.</em>",
        "tag": "please"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>Opens the door.</em> → ✓ <em>Open the door.</em> · ✗ <em>Not run!</em> → ✓ <em>Don't run!</em> · ✗ <em>Be not late.</em> / <em>Don't late.</em> → ✓ <em>Don't be late.</em> · ✗ <em>Let's to go.</em> → ✓ <em>Let's go.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "You see imperatives everywhere: on signs, in recipes and on the road (<em>Turn left. Add two eggs. Mind the gap.</em>). For a softer request, you can also ask a question: <em>Can you open the window, please?</em> You will learn this in <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>. For the order of words in a sentence, see <a href=\"/word-order\">Word order</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
